// From the journal, not yet reached here (spec). Who may see what, where it
// is hosted, and how anyone finds it:
//
// Visibility is a field's own. A thing may say its parent is not visible
// while it is, or the other way around, and one of those is the default.
// Whether a program may reach the network is the same kind of answer, not a
// status somebody reads afterwards. Whether data — or anything derived from
// it, a count of it included — may be sent over the network is a permission
// of its own, given separately from reading it.
//
// Visibility has a visibility: a thing may be findable in an index while
// being unreadable, and a thing may say that even its existence is not to be
// found. Indexes may be turned off, and then what they gave is gone.
//
// Permissions may be for this once, or while a condition holds — while the
// application is running, say. A mouse or a keyboard someone else controls
// is a thing that asks for one.
//
// Code that runs somewhere else runs as somebody. An application may run as
// whoever wrote it, for the permissions that needs, and then `@me` is a
// question: whose code is it, and did I lift it to my own permissions? What
// is asked on the other device is that `@company` wants to run something
// that originated from `@me` — and whether that origin is one name or a
// chain of them.
//
// `@ether` is code like any other: it is allowed here, and not allowed
// elsewhere, and what a local copy of a company's code may read on this disk
// is given to it by name.
//
// Hosting. Rather than running the infrastructure, each person's cloud
// account is held privately for them, with their credentials theirs and at
// most the backups here. That needs two things from a provider: accounts
// made for people without their own, and a third party allowed to commit a
// limited amount to the bill so a free tier is possible. What is free lives
// on an account that invoices directly; moving to a paid setup either shares
// what was free or takes it over, and spending that the person cannot raise
// themselves has to be a thing the provider supports. Providers are shown in
// a random order. Until they integrate directly, this is OAuth. Smaller
// hosts, themselves hosted on the larger ones, are hosts too.
//
// A machine may be given by its details and an ssh login: install there and
// report back. Servers of that kind do not update themselves by default.
//
// Ownership of data by the person, by making version control where the
// central platform is only an index, the way a name server is.
//
// Complicated access costs money, because answering it runs on the
// provider's side.
//
// Finding things. Server discovery, and discovering the ways servers are
// discovered, community-run. The last server logged out of is rejoined
// automatically, per device, with an offer to go to where that device was;
// others are joined from the menu, which remembers where this instance has
// been, or where anyone has.
//
// What is kept on a persona: preferences, the fields they are interested in,
// the knowledge trees already unlocked — and how much of that is generated
// rather than answered.
//
// A stack is an arbitrary graph of a type, so a stack of keys or of
// credentials is one. Whether a graph lets a cursor walk it to find what is
// in it, or asks a persona to walk there themselves, is the graph's own
// answer, and it is the difference between "what is in this room" and "what
// in this room I can reach now". Navigating and storing things in any
// structure is what the infrastructure is for.
//
// Filesystems may be virtual, made into the Ether; a thing may be
// canonicalised as one, with fields delegated to files and directories —
// what is `@me`'s under `@me.ray`, other things like stars elsewhere.
//
// Names. What is done about platforms that already have an `@ether` name
// registered, and whether hardware addresses are reserved too. Separate
// directories for names of different kinds — `orbitmines.com` beside
// `@orbitmines` — mostly to keep what already exists working. Namespaces are
// per project, which is per repository, and each project has its own
// `.ether` holding its configuration.
//
// Events may be injected into somebody else's code by whoever is running it.
//
// In a repl, the setting that makes changes stay is the persona's; a repl
// session may commit everything it changed into one named thing.
//
// Whether an environment variable is what it was at startup or what it is
// now, and whether a static field is shared or is one per user.
//
// A screen, or a place, says what it is allowed to see — `@public` while it
// is being streamed — and what is shown may be conditional on who is
// looking: `if @public`, `elsif` somebody else.
//
// A link keeps a cache that whoever depends on it can publish, for when the
// domain it points at stops answering; publishing something may ask for one,
// so that what fails at runtime has somewhere to fall back to.
//
// Load balancing, from a provider or built in, is also how a new feature is
// given to some of the traffic and not the rest.
//
// Whether what is private lives in a setup of its own, and what mixing it
// with the rest costs when a repository has to be reached on more than one
// machine.
//
// Configuration is `.cfg.ray`: `Cfg /file/path`, `Cfg @me/file`, `Cfg /File`
// or `Cfg.File`, and a name with nothing above it is searched for wherever
// it is. Configuration is code, so it runs. Variables written `&` are the
// ones a context is allowed to change.
//
// Every place normalises differently: what a thing is represented as there
// is that place's answer, not the thing's.
//
// Quests are side quests too: the competencies nobody expected to need, and
// the reasons somebody does a thing because they want to.
//
// Movement through a graph may have to go around what is in it rather than
// through it.
//
// One program, two frontends: what is run from the command line and what is
// shown in an interface are the same code with another hook into it.
